format. (4) The scoped pass MUST also assert a required-identifier presence (`RemoteAddr` here — the fallback chain) AND emit a single 'missing function' hit if the named function is absent entirely, so the gate cannot pass silently when its target is removed by refactor. (5) SECURITY.md is the human-facing co-contract: the test pins (a) a verification-gate row in the Required Verification Gates table (with the `-run <Prefix>` selector — use a single shared test-function name prefix like `TestHTTPServerHardening` so one selector covers all four tests) AND (b) a dedicated section heading AND (c) a closed list of canonical substrings (operator expectations, the closed allow-list of headers BY NAME so a reader cannot mistake the policy, the load-bearing field name like `ReadHeaderTimeout`, and the test file path itself as the link to the enforcement). Watch line breaks in SECURITY.md substrings — a typed prose paragraph reflows and the apostrophe in `operator's` can land on its own line, breaking `strings.Contains`; either rewrap to keep the canonical phrase on a single line (preferred) or split into shorter pinned substrings. (6) The self-check sub-test feeds known-good AND known-bad synthetic fixtures through every matcher — for the struct-literal scan, every required field AND every forbidden field AND every forbidden call selector gets its own isolated regression fixture, plus a multiple-regressions fixture that confirms hits compose additively (e.g. missing-field + forbidden-field + forbidden-call = 3 hits). Forward-applicable to: any future security-verification story whose invariant is 'this typed struct literal in production code MUST/MUST NOT carry field X' or 'this named function MUST consult only allow-listed string literals' (e.g. middleware composition order pins, context-key allow-lists, telemetry attribute redaction key allow-lists, future proxy-trust expansions for X-Forwarded-Proto or RFC 7239 `Forwarded` if Yalla ever adopts them).

